1. Recursion

Recursion is a programming technique in which a method calls itself repeatedly to solve a problem by breaking it into smaller subproblems.

Basic Structure
static void fun(int n) {
    if (condition) {
        return;
    }
    fun(n - 1);
}


Advantages of Recursion:

Makes some problems easier to understand.
Reduces the amount of code for certain problems.
Useful for tree and graph problems.
Useful for divide-and-conquer algorithms.
Essential for many backtracking problems.


Disadvantages of Recursion:

Uses additional stack memory.
Too many recursive calls can cause StackOverflowError in Java.
Sometimes slower than iteration.
Can be difficult to debug.
Incorrect or missing base cases can cause infinite recursion.


Backtracking

Backtracking is an algorithmic technique that builds a solution step by step and removes or undoes a choice when that choice does not lead to a valid solution.
